\par\par \b \uc2  Norway PM says monetary policy to stay unchanged. \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  OSLO, Jan 21 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Kjell Magne Bondevik on Wednesday pledged to stick to Norway's monetary policy of a stable crown against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and urged unions to limit wage demands for 1998. \par \par \uc2  "The existing flexible policy of a fixed rate will stay the same," Bondevik told a news conference. "The partners in the wage negotiations can rely on this." \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund has suggested that Norway should shift monetary policy to a target of limiting inflation, away from the present overriding target of keeping the crown stable against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the existing policy, under which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out swings in the exchange rate, helps unions and safeguards jobs by giving the big export sector greater predictability about future earnings. \par \par \uc2  Bondevik said that, with the "important premise" of a stable crown "we hope and believe that the wage round will succeed. We don't say without turbulence, there can be a good deal of that, but we hope that it will be agreed within an acceptable framework." \par \par \uc2  He said that workers had seen "considerable" hikes in real wages in recent years. \par \par \uc2  "The partners in working life are well served by moderate rises," he said. "Then it's easier to keep inflation and interest rates down and continue the fixed rate policy." \par \par \uc2  Officials say a switch to an inflation target could lead to higher interest rates to dampen growth in a booming oil-fuelled economy, and in turn spur demands for higher wages. \par \par \uc2  The IMF last month said the economy, bolstered by North Sea oil wealth, had begun to overheat and urged Norway either to hike short-term interest rates by about one percentage point or tighten fiscal policy. \par \par \uc2  ((Alister Doyle, Oslo newsroom +47 22 42 50 41, fax +47 22 42 00 18, oslo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1l03zaa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
