\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Mark tumbles on Euro crosses, Norway shines. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 10 (Reuter) - The mark weakened sharply on many of its European crosses on Friday, plunging to its lowest level against the Norwegian crown for more than four years and eroding key levels against the peseta. \par \par \uc2  The backdrop of weak German economic data released on Thursday helped to push the mark lower, while the apparent halting of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Spain sped up the decline, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Many European countries have been locked in a struggle to prevent their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating further in order to avoid losing export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "As it is, the central banks of Norway and Spain appear to have given up the fight for the time being," said Tony Norfield, treasury economist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  But several other central banks were said to have jumped to the mark's defence in European morning trading as its weakness gathered momentum. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Finland sold markkas for marks, while the Bank of Portugal was seen buying marks for escudos. The Swedish Riksbank was also rumoured to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to slow the decline of the mark/Swedish crown rate. \par \par \uc2  The mark's slide accelerated after the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for some time. It had been an aggressive seller of the crown recently to stem its appreciation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The mark/Norwegian crown rate fell to a low of 4.03 per mark before recovering to 4.0470/10 by midsession. At the start of this week the crown stood around 4.13 per mark. \par \par \uc2  The crown is subject to a "dirty float", which means that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a level that is not declared officially. \par \par \uc2  Announcing a temporary policy shift, the Norwegian bank said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to any substantial extent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over a period. \par \par \uc2  The crown's surge forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut official interest rates for the second time this week, lowering the deposit and overnight rates by a quarter of a percentage point to 3.25 and 5.25 percent, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had sold a cumulative 20 billion crowns ($3.1 billion) since the start of 1997. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the mark/peseta rate fell through the key 84 peseta level as the Bank of Spain, which had been defending the mark heavily against the peseta since Wednesday, pulled its bids. \par \par \uc2  The Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been defending the 84 level for several months, they said. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have swollen considerably and rose by $1.64 billion in December to $61.83 billion. \par \par \uc2  The Norwegian crown has become a favourite for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders attracted by the solid fundamentals of the Norwegian economy, such as its huge, oil-related current account and fiscal surpluses, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The recent rise in oil prices has been a boon for the crown as Norway is a key oil producer and exporter. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot on the plus side for the Norwegian crown and a lot of investors are waking up to that," said Norfield. \par \par \uc2  The weak mark theme was reinforced by German data released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It's not solely a Norwegian crown story," said Keith Edmonds, chief analyst at IBJ International in London. "The weak German data helped revive speculation that German interest rates will be cut again." \par \par \uc2  German unemployment rose by 48,000 in December to 4.156 million, or 10.8 percent of the workforce. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product data reinforced perceptions that the German economy would remain in the doldrums for quite some time. \par \par \uc2  GDP rose by 1.4 percent last year after 1.9 percent growth in 1995. The economy stagnated in the final quarter with virtually no growth from the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  -- London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 ($1=6.396 Norwegian Crown) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1a07we8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
